  "letters": [
   {
    "n": 147,
    "year": 1642,
    "date": "April 28, 1642",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 366",
    "excerpt": "The Father Assistant of Spain in a reply of his tells me that the Father Procurator come hither from the West Indies does not want, nor will bring with him, subjects; and so for now there is no occasion to send other workmen — although he adds that another Father Procurator is being awaited, who will come for this purpose; whence he adds to me that on one, or the other occasion I shall be consoled. I wished to write it to Your Paternity so that by her all may be confirmed. Although my desire would be to be consoled as soon as possible, whence I pray and supplicate Your Paternity — for love of that Lord who gives me this desire, and to Your Paternity [a greater one], so that his glory be amplified through the world — that she wish to console me, and send me with the first.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "The Father Assistant of Spain in a reply of his tells me that the Father Procurator come hither from the West Indies does not want, nor will bring with him, subjects; and so for now there is no occasion to send other workmen — although he adds that another Father Procurator is being awaited, who will come for this purpose; whence he adds to me that on one, or the other occasion I shall be consoled. I wished to write it to Your Paternity so that by her all may be confirmed. Although my desire would be to be consoled as soon as possible, whence I pray and supplicate Your Paternity — for love of that Lord who gives me this desire, and to Your Paternity [a greater one], so that his glory be amplified through the world — that she wish to console me, and send me with the first. Our Father, I believe I would die of desire, and of holy envy, if I heard others depart and I remain. There is no doubt that I would know such to be the will of my Lord, but not for this would I not have occasion to weep for my many defects and imperfections, which have retarded me from so great a good. The great charity, and mercy of Your Paternity can cover, and forget these. I hope in the Lord that for the future I shall truly attend to holy mortification: although in many letters I have said this, and then I have not [done it], I commend myself to the prayers and Holy Sacrifices of Your Paternity, so that she obtain from the Lord grace perfectly to vanquish my passions, and to make me a worthy workman in his holy vineyard."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 148,
    "year": 1642,
    "date": "May 5, 1642",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 369",
    "excerpt": "I am awaiting with very great anxiety some letter with which I may console myself, finding myself in that from Your Paternity assured of what I so desire. But my unworthiness has been cause that not only did I not obtain what I desire, but that I was not even esteemed worthy of a reply to so many of mine — for my consolation, so that from Your Paternity, as from the principal fount, I might know all. Yet I content myself, because I know all comes from him who is a most loving Father, whom I pray that for love of the Lord she wish to remember me in the occurrences.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I am awaiting with very great anxiety some letter with which I may console myself, finding myself in that from Your Paternity assured of what I so desire. But my unworthiness has been cause that not only did I not obtain what I desire, but that I was not even esteemed worthy of a reply to so many of mine — for my consolation, so that from Your Paternity, as from the principal fount, I might know all. Yet I content myself, because I know all comes from him who is a most loving Father, whom I pray that for love of the Lord she wish to remember me in the occurrences. Your Paternity, believe me, that neither in the night do I dream of other, nor in the day think of other, than how I may be sent as soon as possible; but since for now no opportunity occurs, at least I pray and supplicate her that, when some opportunity occurs, I be included among those who shall go. Your Paternity forgive me if with continual letters I give her some annoyance, for I burn with desire, nor can I do otherwise; if she wants no more letters, send me this as soon as possible — or at least assure me, Your Paternity, that I shall be among the first — while to her most holy prayers and Sacrifices I commend myself."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 149,
    "year": 1642,
    "date": "May 19, 1642",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 370",
    "excerpt": "From that of Your Paternity I understood that for now there was no occasion to send workmen to the Indies, and therefore there was more need of prayers than of multiplying letters. As to the first, I say that whatever efficacy the prayers of a sinner have before his Divine Majesty, the same have mine — recognizing myself quite unworthy of such grace; but trusting in his infinite mercy, I hope that it will be to console me, and to give occasion to Your Paternity to be able to employ her sons in the aid of those souls.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "From that of Your Paternity I understood that for now there was no occasion to send workmen to the Indies, and therefore there was more need of prayers than of multiplying letters. As to the first, I say that whatever efficacy the prayers of a sinner have before his Divine Majesty, the same have mine — recognizing myself quite unworthy of such grace; but trusting in his infinite mercy, I hope that it will be to console me, and to give occasion to Your Paternity to be able to employ her sons in the aid of those souls. As to the second — that is, of not multiplying letters — I confess the truth: in this I seem to myself to proceed not as a free cause, but a necessary one; for so great is the desire I have that, if I do not find myself already arrived — not to say departed — I shall never cease to supplicate and pray her. Therefore I pray her to have patience, and to bear with one who is indiscreet not because he wishes, but because for his consolation he finds no other means. Father Luigi della Torre wrote to me that, when opportunity of departure occurred, he hoped to make me be one among the others; I would wish that Your Paternity confirm all this with her authority. The principal cause that moved me to this request is that, notified once by Your Paternity, besides that I would be sure of departure, I would have in my judgment more obligation to attend to the acquisition of those virtues necessary for so great a ministry; for perhaps with this spur my great and very great negligence would be excited. This is the principal cause that moves me — while to the prayers and Holy Sacrifices of Your Paternity I commend myself."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 150,
    "year": 1642,
    "date": "May 26, 1642",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 373",
    "excerpt": "Although nothing new occurs to me, yet the present shall serve to remind Your Paternity that, when occasion occurs, she wish to remember me, and console me; for as I have written other times, this is the only, and sole consolation that I hope and desire — nor shall I ever hold myself content except when I see myself already to have obtained the intent: of going and shedding my blood and life in service of my Christ the Lord. For love of God I pray Your Paternity that, when occasion should occur, I be included in the number of those who shall go; and albeit by my little virtue I be unworthy of so great a grace, yet the mercy of the Lord shall supply my failing — for if I had not this firm hope in the mercy of God, I would not have dared to ask of Your Paternity this grace.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Although nothing new occurs to me, yet the present shall serve to remind Your Paternity that, when occasion occurs, she wish to remember me, and console me; for as I have written other times, this is the only, and sole consolation that I hope and desire — nor shall I ever hold myself content except when I see myself already to have obtained the intent: of going and shedding my blood and life in service of my Christ the Lord. For love of God I pray Your Paternity that, when occasion should occur, I be included in the number of those who shall go; and albeit by my little virtue I be unworthy of so great a grace, yet the mercy of the Lord shall supply my failing — for if I had not this firm hope in the mercy of God, I would not have dared to ask of Your Paternity this grace. Therefore I pray her anew to remember me, and commend me to the Lord in her Holy prayers and Sacrifices."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 151,
    "year": 1642,
    "date": "June 16, 1642",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 379",
    "excerpt": "I have heard from some letters that have come from your quarters that it will not be long before Your Paternity sends some men to the Indies. With this letter I first remind him of my request, that he would console me. Secondly, I beg him for love of the Lord not to allow others to go while I am not among them, for I would die of holy envy; for I desire to partake of so great a grace, and also because God knows when there will be another such opportunity. All the more so because, as I have always written to Your Paternity, I have placed myself in his hands, to be sent wherever it pleases Your Paternity to send me.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I have heard from some letters that have come from your quarters that it will not be long before Your Paternity sends some men to the Indies. With this letter I first remind him of my request, that he would console me. Secondly, I beg him for love of the Lord not to allow others to go while I am not among them, for I would die of holy envy; for I desire to partake of so great a grace, and also because God knows when there will be another such opportunity. All the more so because, as I have always written to Your Paternity, I have placed myself in his hands, to be sent wherever it pleases Your Paternity to send me. I pray, then, with all the affection and earnestness I can muster, that he will not let this opportunity pass — all the more so because the Lord, in His mercy, continually gives me occasions in which I see ever more clearly that this is His most holy will. I trust that He Himself will move Your Paternity's heart to send me, and just as in this mission everything comes from His hand, so I trust that He will arrange and dispose things so that I may be consoled. Meanwhile I beg him to obtain for me from the Lord, by his holy prayers and sacrifices, that I may be a worthy instrument of His glory."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 152,
