    "date": "June 30, 1642",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 386",
    "excerpt": "From Father Provincial I received news that Your Paternity had written me into the book of those who ask to go to the Indies; there is no doubt that this was most welcome to me, but to my desire it seems very little — all the more so since, as I hear, the departure will be this September. Wherefore I entreat Your Paternity for love of the Lord that he would console me; and if with this opportunity he can send me, it would be my greatest consolation, and, as I hope and believe, a thing most welcome to the Lord, who in His mercy calls me to this.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "From Father Provincial I received news that Your Paternity had written me into the book of those who ask to go to the Indies; there is no doubt that this was most welcome to me, but to my desire it seems very little — all the more so since, as I hear, the departure will be this September. Wherefore I entreat Your Paternity for love of the Lord that he would console me; and if with this opportunity he can send me, it would be my greatest consolation, and, as I hope and believe, a thing most welcome to the Lord, who in His mercy calls me to this. Our Father, ever since I began writing to him, I have placed myself in his hands like a piece of wood; I stand waiting for the movement toward whatever place pleases him most, as the Lord shall move his heart. I know that Your Paternity does not lack workers more worthy than I — indeed, in myself I recognize no dignity or merit for such a grace and favor — but I ask it all as a mercy, and solely because I know that Your Paternity is wont to aid those who are most wretched and unworthy, it being the property of Christ to make apostles of persecutors and evangelists of usurers. I beg him for love of the Lord to remember me and to console me as soon as possible, while I commend myself to his holy prayers and sacrifices."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 153,
    "year": 1642,
    "date": "July 16, 1642",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 393",
    "excerpt": "If I have let two posts pass without writing to Your Paternity begging to be sent to the Indies, the cause has not been that my desire is less alive now than before; on the contrary, I feel it kindle in me continually, and the Lord in His mercy gives me new incentives. It was that I might not weary Your Paternity with so many letters, and also because of my extraordinary occupations in preparing for the examination for the fourth year of theology. I say this because I take this occasion to remind him that it is now time, if Your Paternity wishes to console me; for whether wholly or not, little remains before I shall be entirely loosed from my studies, so that I may employ myself in the service of our common Lord.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "If I have let two posts pass without writing to Your Paternity begging to be sent to the Indies, the cause has not been that my desire is less alive now than before; on the contrary, I feel it kindle in me continually, and the Lord in His mercy gives me new incentives. It was that I might not weary Your Paternity with so many letters, and also because of my extraordinary occupations in preparing for the examination for the fourth year of theology. I say this because I take this occasion to remind him that it is now time, if Your Paternity wishes to console me; for whether wholly or not, little remains before I shall be entirely loosed from my studies, so that I may employ myself in the service of our common Lord. From my Father Rector I received the greeting Your Paternity sent me; but together with it — though most welcome to me — I would have wished some good and near hope of being consoled as soon as possible, all the more so because, as I hear, the departure will be this year. I beg him for love of the Lord to remember me and to console me as soon as possible. I pray the Lord that in His mercy He may move Your Paternity's heart to console me. I commend myself to his holy sacrifices and prayers."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 154,
    "year": 1642,
    "date": "July 21, 1642",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 397",
    "excerpt": "I humbly thank Your Paternity for the remembrance he keeps of me in his letters to my Father Rector and Father Provincial, but I would that he also remember to console me as soon as possible by sending me to the Indies I so ardently desire. I count the days and hours, and it seems to me they creep by until there comes from Your Paternity the news of departure I desire; with every post I ask what news has come; every letter I believe to be the one that will bear my joy to me, but I always find them empty of it, and I remain with my desire increased.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "I humbly thank Your Paternity for the remembrance he keeps of me in his letters to my Father Rector and Father Provincial, but I would that he also remember to console me as soon as possible by sending me to the Indies I so ardently desire. I count the days and hours, and it seems to me they creep by until there comes from Your Paternity the news of departure I desire; with every post I ask what news has come; every letter I believe to be the one that will bear my joy to me, but I always find them empty of it, and I remain with my desire increased. How many times I lament my negligence, for had it not been boundless, I would now be in possession of so great a good! Often I lament the place where I find myself, so far from Your Paternity that two months must pass before I can have any answer. Gladly, if it pleased Your Paternity, I would change places with someone from the Province of Naples — not for any other reason but that I would find myself readier, and on occasion more prompt to carry out whatever might be commanded me by Your Paternity concerning my petition; I have seen by experience that the nearer men are, the more occasions they have to obtain what they holily desire. Secondly, because this would be a greater consolation to me: being far from relatives and acquaintances, I would have more occasion to dedicate myself and give myself wholly to my Lord until I am called by Your Paternity. I have proposed my desire, but I submit myself to whatever Your Paternity commands me, nor do I desire anything but to be handled and moved by holy obedience. I tell Your Paternity in all sincerity that I do not profess these things as ends in themselves, and what I do I do for no other end than to obtain as soon as possible the desired mission; and so I beg him, and for love of the wounds of Christ I entreat him, to console me, and in his holy prayers and sacrifices to pray the Lord for this unworthy son of his in the Lord."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 155,
    "year": 1642,
    "date": "August 12, 1642",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 400",
    "excerpt": "O God, when will that letter come that will bring infinite consolation and joy to my heart! I am confident, trusting in the mercy of the Lord and in his who is my Father in the Lord, that it is to come and will come without doubt; but what I would wish is that it come as soon as possible. Too heavy is the delay for one who greatly desires. Your Paternity knows, as I have written at other times, that my desire is such that every other thing but this can give me not only no solid joy, but not even, as it seems to me, the beginning of it.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "O God, when will that letter come that will bring infinite consolation and joy to my heart! I am confident, trusting in the mercy of the Lord and in his who is my Father in the Lord, that it is to come and will come without doubt; but what I would wish is that it come as soon as possible. Too heavy is the delay for one who greatly desires. Your Paternity knows, as I have written at other times, that my desire is such that every other thing but this can give me not only no solid joy, but not even, as it seems to me, the beginning of it. If, then, I desire this consolation, why should I not obtain it when I ask it of my Father — all the more so since I am most certain that it is his will, and that in everything that can console and promote his subjects and sons in Christ to perfection he is most ready. I pray, then, that Your Paternity, as soon as possible, will deign to remember me and send me where I am called by the Lord. I commend myself to the prayers and holy sacrifices of Your Paternity."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 156,
    "year": 1642,
    "date": "August 25, 1642",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 401",
    "excerpt": "Blessed be the Lord God, who has willed to console the whole Society, and not least me his unworthy son, with the recovery of Your Paternity's health; I hope in His infinite mercy that He will grant it completely, for the good of the whole Society — which the whole world knows what a Father it would lose. I render new and infinite thanks to the Lord, and I pray Him to diminish my useless life in order to add it to Your Paternity's. With this occasion I also pray him to remember me and to send me where I am called by God; if Your Paternity knew the immense desire I have in my breast for this mission to the Indies, he would certainly send me at once.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Blessed be the Lord God, who has willed to console the whole Society, and not least me his unworthy son, with the recovery of Your Paternity's health; I hope in His infinite mercy that He will grant it completely, for the good of the whole Society — which the whole world knows what a Father it would lose. I render new and infinite thanks to the Lord, and I pray Him to diminish my useless life in order to add it to Your Paternity's. With this occasion I also pray him to remember me and to send me where I am called by God; if Your Paternity knew the immense desire I have in my breast for this mission to the Indies, he would certainly send me at once. I cannot otherwise explain it than by letters. For love of the Lord, let him not forget me in the occasions that arise; and in his holy sacrifices and prayers commend me to the Lord."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 157,
    "year": 1642,
    "date": "September 1, 1642",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 402",
    "excerpt": "If my sins were not what they are, without doubt the Lord God would have by this hour disposed things so that Your Paternity would soon send more workers than one from Sicily, and I would not stand here with this envy — though I know it is a good one — which I bear toward our Father Metello Saccano, called by Your Paternity for Japan. Ever since I learned of his call, the Lord has made me know more fully how imperfect I am, having seen clearly that those are chosen by Him who render themselves more worthy by the exercise of the virtues.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "If my sins were not what they are, without doubt the Lord God would have by this hour disposed things so that Your Paternity would soon send more workers than one from Sicily, and I would not stand here with this envy — though I know it is a good one — which I bear toward our Father Metello Saccano, called by Your Paternity for Japan. Ever since I learned of his call, the Lord has made me know more fully how imperfect I am, having seen clearly that those are chosen by Him who render themselves more worthy by the exercise of the virtues. Yet for all this I shall not cease to pray Your Paternity to send me, for I hope in the Lord that He will give me such grace as will supply my defect, since He has called me and continually calls me. Is it possible that I shall not obtain from Your Paternity what I know most certainly He relishes and wishes to grant me? I make no condition about the mission: wherever he sends me I shall go most readily. This I desire, this I long for, this I think on day and night; this I shall ask until either death comes or I am sent by Your Paternity for love of the Lord. I beg him to remember me, and in his holy sacrifices and prayers to commend me to the Lord."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 158,
    "year": 1642,
    "date": "September 15, 1642",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 743, 411",
    "excerpt": "Until now I have lived, as the saying goes, on hope, believing that this year I should be consoled by Your Paternity and sent to my Indies; but since it has not pleased the Lord that it be so, may His most holy will be done. At least I pray Your Paternity to assure me that he will send me with the first mission made after this one, and with the first who go. Today, finding myself precisely in the holy Exercises, I was thinking what means I might take to obtain from Your Paternity as soon as possible what I so ardently desire; but finding nothing to lay hold of, I chose — cast at Your Paternity's feet, since it is not lawful for me to do so in person and with the body — to do it with the spirit and the heart, and to beg him for love of the Lord to console me, for otherwise I shall die of pure desire.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Until now I have lived, as the saying goes, on hope, believing that this year I should be consoled by Your Paternity and sent to my Indies; but since it has not pleased the Lord that it be so, may His most holy will be done. At least I pray Your Paternity to assure me that he will send me with the first mission made after this one, and with the first who go. Today, finding myself precisely in the holy Exercises, I was thinking what means I might take to obtain from Your Paternity as soon as possible what I so ardently desire; but finding nothing to lay hold of, I chose — cast at Your Paternity's feet, since it is not lawful for me to do so in person and with the body — to do it with the spirit and the heart, and to beg him for love of the Lord to console me, for otherwise I shall die of pure desire. O how many times have I wished for wings, that I might in person plead my case before Your Paternity and vent to him the ardor I have in my breast! I have placed myself in the hands of holy obedience: what do I desire but that I be set by it, wherever it says and judges? This I ask, this I seek, and either I shall obtain it or die of pure desire. And so I pray him anew, for the love of God, who communicates this desire to me, to console me and to commend me to the Lord in his holy prayers and sacrifices."
    ]
   },
   {
    "n": 160,
    "year": 1643,
    "date": "April 6, 1643",
    "place": "Palermo",
    "archival": "ARSI, FG 744, 57",
    "excerpt": "Our Father, on the one hand I have nothing to write except to pray him for what I have continually prayed him; on the other I cannot but write, for so great is the desire I have to obtain this grace from Your Paternity that, were I to let some post pass without writing, it would seem to me I had committed a very great fault, and that I did not correspond to the great benefits of the Lord. Besides, the desire itself gives me no rest unless I pray him continually, as I have done, and more often I would do it if I had more often the opportunity.",
    "kind": "abridged",
    "full_paras": [
     "Our Father, on the one hand I have nothing to write except to pray him for what I have continually prayed him; on the other I cannot but write, for so great is the desire I have to obtain this grace from Your Paternity that, were I to let some post pass without writing, it would seem to me I had committed a very great fault, and that I did not correspond to the great benefits of the Lord. Besides, the desire itself gives me no rest unless I pray him continually, as I have done, and more often I would do it if I had more often the opportunity. For love of the Lord, let Your Paternity remember me in the occasions that arise, and console me as soon as the occasion comes; and I hope in the great charity of Your Paternity, which I know rejoices when it gives its sons occasion of consolation. I commend myself to his holy sacrifices and prayers."
    ]
   }
  ]
